WEBVTT

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.170
A written rest tells a pianist to stop,

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:06.930
but it doesn't tell them for how long.

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:10.070
This demo measures what an energy-based detector

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:11.970
finds inside the Grave opening

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:14.390
of the Pathétique Sonata and how far that

00:00:14.390 --> 00:00:16.930
measurement sits from what the performer actually did.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:23.360
[the author's recording plays]

00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:25.310
Each pause is split into two spans.

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:27.710
The blue span, d_s, is the sounding time

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:30.470
before the detector's threshold is crossed, and the

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:32.950
red span, d_l, is how long the signal

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:34.250
stays below that threshold.

00:00:34.490 --> 00:00:38.130
For the pause you just heard, d_l is 0.380 seconds.

00:00:38.470 --> 00:00:40.810
Both are detector outputs, and neither is a

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:42.370
measurement of musical intention.

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:46.420
My hypothesis here was that the second pause

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:48.140
is systematically the longest one.

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:52.720
Across 16 professional recordings, that idea was not supported.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:55.840
Actually, the longest pause falls on P2 in

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:58.400
9 of the recordings and P3 in 7

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:00.460
of them, which is a two-sided binomial

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:07.720
p-value of 0.80. The paired P2–P3 value, the difference between them, is

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:15.438
minus 0.49 seconds, with a 95% confidence interval from minus 1.42 to plus 0.43.

00:01:15.438 --> 00:01:18.180
Of course, this contains zero, so

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:20.640
it's not a statistically significant result.

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:24.460
Across all four pauses, the ordering is moderate,

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:27.900
with a Kendall's W of 0.46. Removing

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:31.983
pause 4, and the Kendall's value drops to 0.14,

00:01:31.983 --> 00:01:37.200
so the ordering is closest to the single statement that the final pause is

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.830
the shortest. The null matters because the detector uses an

00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:46.430
absolute decibel threshold, which makes it sensitive to how loud the file actually is.

00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:49.730
If you normalize all 16 files to minus

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:54.590
1 dBFS, it moves the within-recording P2

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:57.450
–P3 difference by about 0.25 seconds

00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:01.210
on average, and by 3.62 seconds in one recording.

00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:04.149
The effect I was measuring is 0.49 seconds.

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:06.950
At this precision level, a claim about which

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:09.790
middle pause is longer is also a claim

00:02:09.790 --> 00:02:12.670
about file level and mastering, so this control

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:15.770
reports aggregate metadata only, and it does

00:02:15.770 --> 00:02:19.170
not rerun the selected event or let me

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:20.770
pick a more favorable setting.

00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:25.830
Everything so far rests on one unmeasured assumption.

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:28.450
That is, where the energy falls below the

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:30.730
threshold is where the pianist stopped.

00:02:31.130 --> 00:02:33.730
On a piano, it can't always be like

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:37.450
this because the strings do keep ringing after the key gets released.

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:41.640
So what I did is I recorded the

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:47.280
opening myself, and I captured both audio and MIDI data.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:49.420
The MIDI note-off is the moment my

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:51.840
finger left the key, and the detector boundary

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:54.200
is what the audio algorithm reports here.

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:58.700
And the difference is the error I couldn't

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:00.180
see in the commercial recordings.

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:07.680
Across the four pauses, the boundary lands between 0.056 and 0.188 seconds after

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:11.060
the aligned note-off, which is about 0.11 seconds.

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:16.290
It's late in every single pause, so consistently

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:19.330
that's what the damped string decay is predicting.

00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:25.210
Although there are two caveats, the first is this is just a performance by one pianist,

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:27.310
and it's of course excluded from all the

00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:28.870
figures and the statistics.

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:32.390
The second thing is that the two files

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:34.410
weren't started on the same clock, so the

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:41.790
pipeline actually declares a constant offset of about 4.358 seconds with about 30 milliseconds of uncertainty.

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:45.910
That is the noise floor on a 0.11 second measurement.

00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:53.210
Overall, the statistics and figures regenerate from the

00:03:53.210 --> 00:03:56.530
shipped CSVs with no audio, and regenerating the

00:03:56.530 --> 00:03:58.870
event table, the boundary table, or the waveform

00:03:58.870 --> 00:04:01.570
panels, you do need to download your own

00:04:01.570 --> 00:04:05.630
local copy, because the corpus recordings are commercial

00:04:05.630 --> 00:04:08.070
and cannot be redistributed freely.

00:04:08.350 --> 00:04:13.710
The one playable file in my demo is of course my own performance.

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:24.860
[the author's recording plays]

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:28.660
Overall, the contribution is a small reproducible inspection

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:34.720
interface for active silence, and also one measured estimate of how far an energy threshold

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:36.320
sits from a real key release.

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:41.960
A next step could possibly be replacing order-selected event recovery with a score alignment process,

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:44.380
so the pauses are located by the score

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:46.320
instead of by their position in time.
